{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/GNSS Constellation Redundancy.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/GNSS Constellation Redundancy.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"gnss constellation redundancy gnss constellation redundancy","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/GNSS Constellation Redundancy.md","fileName":"GNSS Constellation Redundancy.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"GNSS Constellation Redundancy","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-21 00:56\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[GPS Accuracy Integrity and Resilience]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["GPS Accuracy Integrity and Resilience","gps.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/GNSS Constellation Redundancy.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/GNSS Constellation Redundancy.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"gnss constellation redundancy gnss constellation redundancy","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/GNSS Constellation Redundancy.md","fileName":"GNSS Constellation Redundancy.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"GNSS Constellation Redundancy","heading":"GNSS Constellation Redundancy","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"GNSS constellation redundancy uses independent providers and extra satellites so that a receiver can continue navigating when one vehicle or system is unavailable. GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, and BeiDou offer different operational control and orbital populations, improving availability and providing some political independence. Their shared reliance on weak L-band signals, spaceborne clocks, and similar orbits means they can still suffer common-mode interference, so multiplicity is not the same as a dissimilar backup.","tags":["baby"],"links":["GPS Accuracy Integrity and Resilience","gps.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/GNSS Constellation Redu